<h2>Key Concepts</h2>

<h3>Gin Context Injection</h3>
<p>Handlers receive <code>*gin.Context</code>, which carries request state (user, tenant, request ID, headers). This allows middleware to inject data that handlers read.</p>

<h3>Unmarshaling & Binding</h3>
<p><code>c.ShouldBindJSON()</code> combines unmarshaling and validation in one call. Errors are caught early.</p>

<h3>Layering Discipline</h3>
<p>Handlers must <strong>not</strong> access the database directly. They must call services. This maintains separation of concerns and makes unit tests faster.</p>

<h2>Common Pitfalls</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Too much logic in handlers:</strong> Handlers should be <5 KB. Move complex logic to services.</li>
<li><strong>Forgetting to validate input:</strong> Always call <code>ShouldBindJSON()</code> or equivalent. Don't trust client input.</li>
<li><strong>Inconsistent error responses:</strong> Return a consistent error schema. Document it in OpenAPI.</li>
<li><strong>Not checking authorization:</strong> Every handler that accesses user data must verify ownership via tenant or user ID.</li>
<li><strong>Direct database access:</strong> Handlers should never call DAO. That's the service's job.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common Patterns</h2>

<h3>Request ID Tracking</h3>
<p>Every request gets a unique ID in middleware. This ID flows through logs, allowing you to trace a single request through all systems.</p>

<pre><code>func LoggingMiddleware() gin.HandlerFunc {
    return func(c *gin.Context) {
        requestID := uuid.New().String()
        c.Set("request_id", requestID)
        c.Header("X-Request-ID", requestID)
        
        c.Next()
        
        latency := time.Since(start)
        logger.Info("request", "request_id", requestID, "latency_ms", latency.Milliseconds())
    }
}
</code></pre>

<h3>Timeout & Cancellation</h3>
<p>All handlers pass context.Context down to services and DAOs. Set a timeout in middleware so slow requests don't accumulate.</p>

<pre><code>func TimeoutMiddleware(timeout time.Duration) gin.HandlerFunc {
    return func(c *gin.Context) {
        ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(c.Request.Context(), timeout)
        defer cancel()
        c.Request = c.Request.WithContext(ctx)
        c.Next()
    }
}
</code></pre>

<h3>Graceful Shutdown</h3>
<p>When the server shuts down, drain in-flight requests before closing connections.</p>

<pre><code>server := &http.Server{Addr: ":8080", Handler: router}

go func() {
    <-shutdown // signal channel
    ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 5*time.Second)
    defer cancel()
    server.Shutdown(ctx)  // wait up to 5s for requests to finish
}()

server.ListenAndServe()
</code></pre>

<h2>Testing Handlers</h2>

<p>Use Gin's test utilities to mock HTTP requests:</p>

<pre><code>func TestSearchHandler(t *testing.T) {
    router := gin.Default()
    mockService := &MockSearchService{}
    handler := &SearchHandler{searchService: mockService}
    
    router.POST("/search", handler.Search)
    
    body := `{"query": "test", "kb_id": "kb1", "top_k": 10}`
    req, _ := http.NewRequest("POST", "/search", strings.NewReader(body))
    w := httptest.NewRecorder()
    
    router.ServeHTTP(w, req)
    
    assert.Equal(t, 200, w.Code)
}
</code></pre>


<h2>Response Formatting Best Practices</h2>

<h3>Standard Response Envelope</h3>
<p>Wrap all responses in a consistent envelope for client-side consistency:</p>

<pre><code>type APIResponse struct {
    Code    int         `json:"code"`      // 0 = success, non-zero = error
    Message string      `json:"message"`   // human-readable message
    Data    interface{} `json:"data"`      // actual response data
    Meta    struct {
        RequestID string `json:"request_id"`
        Timestamp int64  `json:"timestamp"`
    } `json:"meta"`
}

// Usage
c.JSON(200, APIResponse{
    Code: 0,
    Message: "OK",
    Data: searchResult,
    Meta: {
        RequestID: c.GetString("request_id"),
        Timestamp: time.Now().Unix(),
    },
})
</code></pre>

<h3>Pagination Support</h3>
<p>For list endpoints, support offset/limit pagination:</p>

<pre><code>type ListRequest struct {
    Offset int `json:"offset" binding:"gte=0"`
    Limit  int `json:"limit" binding:"gte=1,lte=100"`
}

// Query
var items []Item
d.db.Offset(offset).Limit(limit).Find(&items)

// Response includes total count
type ListResponse struct {
    Items []Item `json:"items"`
    Total int    `json:"total"`
}
</code></pre>

<h3>Error Response Standards</h3>
<p>Provide detailed error information for debugging:</p>

<pre><code>type ErrorResponse struct {
    Code      int    `json:"code"`
    Message   string `json:"message"`
    Details   string `json:"details,omitempty"`    // internal error details
    Timestamp int64  `json:"timestamp"`
    Path      string `json:"path"`
}

// Example: Missing required field
c.JSON(400, ErrorResponse{
    Code: 400,
    Message: "validation error",
    Details: "field 'kb_id' is required",
    Path: c.Request.URL.Path,
    Timestamp: time.Now().Unix(),
})
</code></pre>


